("5. In many cases, text mismatches or attribute differences are **not** treated as fatal: in dev React warns; in production behavior is \"not guaranteed to be patched\".\n\n⚠ The exact granularity (\"nearest boundary vs root\"), which categories (tag vs text vs attribute) are fatal, and the exact warnings changed across 17 → 18 → 19.",
 "5. **Attribute** differences are the one common category that is *not* fatal: in dev React logs a warning (\"A tree hydrated but some attributes of the server rendered HTML didn't match the client properties. This won't be patched up\"); in production nothing is logged and the server's attribute value simply stays. **Text and element (tag/missing/extra node) mismatches are errors** in both dev and production: React reports a recoverable error (production: minified error #418) and client-renders the nearest Suspense boundary, or the whole root if there is none.\n\nExecuted on react-dom 19.3.1 (`renderToString` into jsdom, then `hydrateRoot`, `NODE_ENV` development and production): text mismatch, extra server element and missing server element each produced one recoverable error and the DOM ended up with the *client* output with no server nodes reused (no boundary); the same text mismatch inside `<Suspense>` re-rendered only that boundary (the sibling `<h1>` node was reused); a `className` mismatch left the server's `class` in place with no recoverable error; `suppressHydrationWarning` on an element with a differing text child kept the server text and reported nothing.\n\nThe exact granularity, which categories are fatal, and the exact warnings changed across 17 → 18 → 19."),
("lazy` components inside boundaries can be hydrated after their chunks load without blocking everything else. ⚠ This is an architectural result described in the React 18 working group; verify with an experiment in your framework (§5.6).",
 "lazy` components inside boundaries can be hydrated after their chunks load without blocking everything else. This is an architectural result described in the React 18 working group; executed on 19.3.1 (server HTML for a boundary whose `lazy` child had not loaded stayed in the DOM, no fallback, until the chunk resolved, after which the button was interactive). Confirm in your framework with the experiment in §5.6."),
("2. If the listener exists but the target's boundary is not hydrated, React can prioritize hydrating that boundary; whether the original event is **replayed** afterwards is the unspecified behavior flagged above.",
 "2. If the listener exists but the target's boundary is not hydrated, React hydrates that boundary synchronously for a discrete event and then dispatches the event if hydration succeeded; if it cannot hydrate yet, the discrete event is dropped (see the verified note at the top). It is not replayed later."),
("React notices the event target is inside dehydrated boundary B → **raises B's hydration priority**, hydrates B ahead of C. Then the handler may run (via replay) or the click may be lost (⚠ unspecified). |",
 "React notices the event target is inside dehydrated boundary B → **hydrates B synchronously, ahead of C**, then dispatches the click to B's now-attached handler (a discrete event is not queued for replay; had B been unable to hydrate, the click would be dropped). |"),
("- The text node mismatches → warning in dev; React does **not patch text** (docs). In production the client keeps whichever text is in the DOM (the server's) until a re-render; with recovery paths, React may instead client-render the enclosing boundary/root. The user may see stale text or flicker. Fix options in §5.3.",
 "- The text node mismatches → a recoverable hydration error (dev message \"Hydration failed because the server rendered text didn't match the client\"; production error #418), and React discards the server DOM for the nearest Suspense boundary (or the whole root) and client-renders it. Executed on 19.3.1 in dev and production: the final DOM showed the *client's* time. The user sees a re-render/flicker and pays for the throw-away work. (The server text stays only for an element carrying `suppressHydrationWarning`.) Fix options in §5.3."),
